applications that help us reduce the  repetitive tasks make us more productive  but building them is hard since it  stitches a lot of different applications  together  to explore one such application i've  invited david ting senior vice president  of engineering at knights a company  that's revolutionizing the workflow  automation by connecting data from  multiple applications compiling those  insights and then creating an end-to-end  automation workflow hi david welcome to  the show hi priyanka thank you for  having me  i am so excited to have you on the show  uh tell us a little bit about you and  nylis  i am the svp of engineering and i'm  responsible for nihilist product and  technical execution  natalie's mission is to make the world  more productive and we built up a very  scalable and secure platform to hand  commute handle communication data  driving business process automation okay  so how does it matter to me you as end  users if there are enough developers to  write all the applications you need to  automate and streamlined workflow the  world be much different today why would  i have to enter receive for expenses why  is it so hard to juggle my calendar when  i just need two hours of focus time  building applications to reduce  repetitive tasks are really hard and  nice mission is to make it easier for  you to build high ri application make  you a star in your work wow productivity  sounds really cool and yes i do not want  to get my to upload my receipts into a  system it should just happen  automatically  how does this actually work  so we capture real-time data from  service providers such as gmail  microsoft imap  we have data pipelines power ml models  behind the scene to create intelligent  data objects data from these insights  trigger programmable workflows and the  challenge is building up a distributed  real-time architecture for billions of  messages that we receive each day and  keep the end-to-end latency low and  predictable  all right so now that we have put the  customer expectation in perspective and  now that we know how nihilis gathers the  data and creates these insights  what were the technical drivers for  working with google cloud we came to  google with what we viewed as a very  challenging mission we have a large  enterprise customer in the financial  services space with very strict security  requirements pii can not be stored  persistently and the lifetime of each  server needs to be short to avoid  potential in-memory hacks this was a  great reason for us to rethink the  analysis architecture  the legacy nylon architecture is built  with python flasks on top of aws was  difficult and very costly to scale and  the end to end performance is unreliable  interesting why was it so costly to  scale  nyls processes over 20 terabytes of data  every day and the high volume of read  and writes are mainly hitting against  hundreds of my sequel shards the  workload is partitioned as email  accounts across a farm with a static  assignment because volume of the emails  is unpredictable for each account there  are many hot spots that emerge  throughout the day and causing high  variability and latency and some  insevere cases reliability issues  in order to address this we have to over  provision the system by good margin in  order to keep the latency in control  based on extensive load testing with the  same traffic load whenever we double the  infrastructure footprint we can lower  the p90 latency in half and at our  current scale doubling our footprint  is really means like a million dollar in  cloud cost uh so that's not a  sustainable strategy wow that is a lot  of money  all right so three big major issues that  you set out to solve the security  scalability performance and obviously  cost  so why did you pick google cloud  in my mind google cloud has the best  distributed technology has been a true  engineering partner throughout the  journey  the security scalability and stability  of the platform is second to none gke is  currently the best option out of any  cloud provider to run kubernetes  workload securely at scale and one of  the most performant data stores in  spanner so now i want to get into my  favorite part which is the architecture  how did you approach it what was it like  and how is it going we're able to  transition to a brand new architecture  and achieve a huge roi for the project  the big bets of using gke to handle the  container orchestration pumps up for  message bus and spanner for relational  data store has paid off handsomely we  went live on june 4th  and we really knocked it out of the park  everything has been scaling effectively  for example one application has  performed over  1 billion transaction in less than a  month and then this is on top of spanner  and what i cannot believe is is that uh  the latency is actually controlled very  well to seven cells millisecond and this  really speaks volumes to robustness of  gcp services  by working closely with the gcp account  team  and the great partner in redspin were  able to address most of the issues  before the launch so our customers qa  team went in and tested our initial  prototype they found zero issue  and we spent the rest of the time  optimizing the workload as we scale on  gcp great well how did you go from you  mentioned that you went from python  based application to go you did an  entire rewrite  how did you do that and  um moving from virtual machine instances  on ec2 to gke how did that process  happen  as part of the reimagine imagination  exercise we have to take some big bets  and what we did was we actually decided  to rewrite our services and go  because we believe that there's a 10x  throughput improvement when we when we  do this the second part is moving  basically the orchestration tier  from cloud specific and um  and kind of like an ews auto scaler  group we basically want to go with the  industry standard in gke or kubernetes  so we use gke to orchestrate the  containers and due to the security  requirements the nodes have to be cycled  very quickly so we're able to take  advantage of the fact that we can have  up to 15 000 nodes in the kubernetes  cluster on gke and that's actually  something that other cloud providers  don't provide in addition we're also  using gvisor to run the containers to  create a strong isolation between the  application and operating system this  helps us to lock down the host memory  and storage access and enforce lease  permission principle at the operating  system level earlier you mentioned 20  plus terabytes of data being processed  in hundreds of my sequel shards which  obviously wasn't really efficient and  was also extremely costly  how did you solve that as you reimagined  this architecture in google cloud we  made some big bets and so we used  spanner as the relational data store to  keep states for the application and also  store the keys  for each one of the accounts we need to  connect to  so faster  read and write access performance  extremely critical for a large number of  calls  we need the consistent high performance  they have predictable end-to-end latency  and and that bet actually has paid off  by betting on running on gcp services  versus doing it ourselves  lastly nyls does not have the whole pii  so this information is pushed so that  once  our customers receive it we purge the  information from our store  for security reasons for communication  between those micro services  you used pumps up can we talk a little  bit about  why and how pops up has been helpful  yes so we went from a very  database-centric design to event-centric  design so for us the message bus is a  key in the heart of our  whole design so we chose google pub sub  for event driven microservices  architecture  and just as a comparison in the aws  world in the legacy architecture because  it's actually more of a data-driven  architecture  the uh the data is written into dynamo  then it gets converted to by atlanta  function and then it goes to kinesis  event stream  so in google cloud we want to simplify  this so we want event first and then so  we re wrote it directly on google pub  sub so it actually puts a lot of string  on the system so there's a lot of trust  that we have in how the scales and how  it's going to perform  reliably and uh consistently and we're  very happy with the experience on google  pubs where the performance latency is  minimal even when we have to burst to  tens of thousands of right per second  so we even can even think about eventbus  uh to basically be like a data store for  us because it is that reliable and  scalable for us  yeah that is amazing to hear what are  the  what are some of the benefits that  you've achieved after migrating to  google cloud  this could not happen without  exceptional service so we're very happy  with everyone we worked with  at gcp  we're able to get over 30 times the  throughput per node after the rewriting  go and in addition the elasticity of the  architecture has been amazing it's  reliable latency doesn't go down  performance is actually stable as we  actually has traffic spike it's just  outright amazing when you work with the  high scale architecture  our customer has been migrating their  entire user base onto annihilus in june  and the process has been extremely  smooth and in addition during amazon  prime day where there's a lot of  burst in e-commerce transaction come in  within a minute we saw the number of  nodes went from like a handful to tens  of thousands of nodes like within a few  minutes  and we're able to handle the peak  consistently and the p90 performance  actually was stable even on with those  spikes and it's just really amazing to  think back and we're able to accomplish  out all of that in just three months wow  three months is a pretty impressive  timeline for a migration this big where  you had to rewrite from  from  one language to another in this case  python to go and then um and then also  learning all these new tools  gk spanner and  npub sub and i'm sure it was a very  massive team effort what was your  experience like working with the google  team and the partner in this process  i want to first thank the nihilus  engineering team i have not worked with  a group that is talented and is  dedicated in their work in my career  secondly i would like to thank our  partner gcp investment for making this  happen  we're able to work as one team over  emails and slack and resolve issues in  real time  most companies need at least one year to  pull off an architectural overhaul of  disorder and magnitude  this is absolutely phenomenal the speed  the collaboration  what is the road ahead what does future  technical enhancement look like for your  architecture  with the success we experienced we want  to shoot bigger  so  we have the confidence to handle more  challenges ahead and we're looking to  work with uh gcp on advanced ai ml use  cases where we're extracting valuable  insight contained within communication  channels automating workflows for our  clients  our customers has planned to increase  their usage several orders of magnitude  and they're pushing with all their  marketing power to reach more users and  customers and of course we have many  more mega clients waiting in our  pipeline and we have the confidence with  the partnership that we have to  basically  take out more of them concurrently well  all that sounds really exciting and i  wish you and the team good luck in  implementing some of those enhancements  thanks david for diving into the details  of this incredible journey and  architecture with me well thank you for  the opportunity to  share our experience in nihilus  well we just saw how nihilus migrated to  gke pub sub and spanner to achieve the  performance scalability and security  goals while keeping the costs in check  if you want to learn more check out the  blog i've linked below and if you got  any topic 